CHROMIUM: drm/rockchip: do not expose "Content Protection" on DP and HDMI

Neither dw-dp nor dw-hdmi-qp can complete HDCP on these boards: the
HDCP 2.x controller/ESM firmware is never provisioned and vendor
storage holds no HDCP 1.x key, so every authentication attempt fails.
The enable paths nevertheless commit hardware state before trying
(dw-dp asks BL31 for HDCP encrypt mode, dw-hdmi-qp clears
HDCP2_BYPASS) and only undo it on a success that never comes.

Chrome writes Content Protection=DESIRED on HDMI/DP displays for any
protected (Widevine) playback, which is enough to trigger this: the
output turns solid green and stays so across hotplug and after the
protection request is withdrawn, until reboot.

Only software (L3) Widevine is supported on these boards, so HDCP is
never needed. Drop the property from both connectors; Chrome then
reports no protectable link and playback continues unprotected, while
licenses that require HDCP become output-restricted as intended.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-dp.c
index d26c01ce8..3999b5c44 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-dp.c
@@ -4237,12 +4237,6 @@ static int dw_dp_connector_init(struct dw_dp *dp)
 	dp->color_format_capacity = prop;
 	drm_object_attach_property(&connector->base, prop, 0);
 
-	ret = drm_connector_attach_content_protection_property(&dp->connector, true);
-	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(dp->dev, "failed to attach content protection: %d\n", ret);
-		return ret;
-	}
-
 	prop = drm_property_create_range(connector->dev, 0, RK_IF_PROP_ENCRYPTED,
 					 RK_IF_HDCP_ENCRYPTED_NONE, RK_IF_HDCP_ENCRYPTED_LEVEL2);
 	if (!prop) {
